<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-relations/foundational/what-public-relations-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Public relations is the management discipline of relationships between organisations and
    their publics — built, in its modern form, on research, honest communication and mutual
    influence. The old caricature (press agent plus smoke machine) describes the profession's
    earliest stage and its worst practitioners, not its definition. The Public Relations
    Society of America's current definition — "a strategic communication process that builds
    mutually beneficial relationships between organizations and their publics" — marks the
    shift: from publicity to <em>relationships</em>, from messages to <em>strategy</em>, and
    from outputs (clippings, impressions) to outcomes (trust, permission to operate). This
    unit maps the profession's actual shape: what it is, how it differs from its neighbours
    (advertising, marketing), whom it actually works for (publics, plural), and how its
    four channel families fit together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does the discipline exist at all? Because organisations live on <strong>legitimacy</strong>
    — the ongoing social permission to operate — and legitimacy is granted by publics the
    organisation cannot command: journalists who choose whether to cover it, regulators,
    employees who decide whether to believe its promises, communities that accept or resist
    its presence. Every organisation communicates regardless; PR is the professionalisation of
    that communication into strategy — with the corresponding ethical weight, because a
    profession that shapes public discourse has obligations to the discourse.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four channel families (PESO)" widget="equationStep" config={pesoCfg} />

  <h2>Definitions and history: from press agentry to relationships</h2>
  <p>
    The profession's history explains its caricature and its cure. The late nineteenth-century
    stage was <strong>press agentry</strong> — publicity at any price, epitomised by showmen
    whose motto was accuracy optional. The turn came in the 1900s, when corporate America's
    trust-busting scandals demanded something better. <strong>Ivy Ledbetter Lee</strong>, often
    called the father of modern PR, published his <em>Declaration of Principles</em> (1906):
    the public should be informed; the press should have open access; work should be done in
    the open. His handling of the Pennsylvania Railroad's 1906 derailment — inviting
    journalists to the scene, issuing accurate statements — demonstrated that candour could be
    strategy, and his counsel to the Rockefellers after the 1914 Ludlow Massacre showed that
    listening (even belatedly) beat silence. <strong>Edward Bernays</strong>, Freud's nephew,
    professionalised the persuasion side: he wrote the field's first textbooks (<em>Crystallizing
    Public Opinion</em>, 1923), taught its first university course, and coined "counsel on
    public relations" — while also staging campaigns (the famous 1929 "torches of freedom"
    walk, which rebranded cigarettes as women's liberation) that embody both the power and the
    ethical peril of engineered consent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modern synthesis arrived with James Grunig and Todd Hunt's <em>Managing Public
    Relations</em> (1984) and the long "excellence study" that followed. Their four models —
    press agentry, public information, two-way asymmetric, two-way <strong>symmetrical</strong> —
    describe the profession's evolution as a progression toward dialogue: excellent practice
    researches its publics, negotiates with them, and lets the organisation be changed by what
    it hears, because relationships built on one-way persuasion do not survive contact with a
    sceptical public. The excellence research found the symmetrical ideal correlates with
    effectiveness (and critics rightly note real practice often falls short of it), but as a
    <em>definition</em> it holds the field's centre: PR is two-way, research-based and
    relationship-constituting — which is why measurement migrated from clip counts to trust
    and outcomes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that PR means spin — deceptive reframing of facts. Practically, spin that unravels is a <em>crisis</em>, and crisis response is PR's most scrutinised product; the profession's hard-won lesson is that honesty is a strategy rather than a virtue tax. The ethical spine is codified (the PRSA Code: advocacy, honesty, expertise, independence, loyalty, fairness) precisely because the profession's failure mode — misleading publics — destroys the only asset it manages.</p>

  <h2>PR versus advertising and marketing: control, credibility and channel families</h2>
  <p>
    The cleanest distinction is <strong>control versus credibility</strong>. Advertising buys
    guaranteed placement for fully controlled messages: you know what runs, where and when —
    and audiences discount it accordingly, because everyone knows it was paid for. PR earns
    third-party validation it cannot fully control: a journalist's story, an expert's citation,
    a customer's review carry credibility precisely <em>because</em> the organisation did not
    buy them — and carry risk advertising never touches, since the story may run against you.
    Marketing, meanwhile, is the larger commercial discipline of moving products to customers
    (the four Ps: product, price, place, promotion); PR is broader than its promotional slice
    because it serves relationships with <em>all</em> publics — investors, regulators,
    employees, communities — including publics who will never buy anything. Modern practice
    integrates the three, and the working map is the <strong>PESO model</strong> (Gini
    Dietrich): <strong>Paid</strong> (advertising, sponsored content — precise and scalable,
    discounted in credibility and increasingly disclosure-bound), <strong>Earned</strong>
    (media coverage you did not pay for — credible because a third party chose it), <strong>Shared</strong>
    (social conversation, reviews, shares — participation you can join but not command),
    <strong>Owned</strong> (your site, blog, newsletter — full control, lower trust, and the
    backbone the other channels point back to). The widget above walks the four; the strategic
    skill is orchestration — moving one true story across families so each does what it does
    best.
  </p>
  <p>
    Measurement is where the distinction became enforceable. The industry's legacy metric —
    advertising value equivalency (valuing a news story as if the space were bought) — was
    formally discredited by the <strong>Barcelona Principles</strong> (first declared 2010,
    reaffirmed since): measure <em>outcomes</em> (behaviour, trust, relationship quality), not
    <em>outputs</em> (impressions, clip counts); PR's contribution is captured in surveys of
    awareness and trust, share of credible voice, stakeholder sentiment, and ultimately in
    whether the organisation retains its licence to operate. A worked contrast: a product
    launch buys reach (advertising's job), courts reviews and analyst coverage (earned),
    hosts the canonical facts on its own site (owned) and monitors community reaction
    (shared) — and the PR team's report is not "we reached 4 million people" but "trusted
    third-party coverage reached the audiences advertising cannot convince, and sentiment held
    through the launch week".
  </p>

  <h2>Stakeholders and publics: the plural that defines the field</h2>
  <p>
    An organisation never has one audience, and PR's first analytical act — before any writing
    happens — is identifying and prioritising its <strong>publics</strong>. Employees,
    investors, regulators, journalists, customers, suppliers, neighbours, NGOs and activist
    groups each hold different stakes, use different channels and bring different scepticism;
    a message that reassures investors (efficiency gains) can enrage employees (their jobs) in
    the same sentence. Grunig's <strong>situational theory of publics</strong> supplies the
    segmentation logic: a public forms around a problem when three conditions align — people
    <em>recognise</em> the problem, <em>feel involved</em> in it, and believe they are
    <em>not constrained</em> from acting on it. The same layoff announcement therefore produces
    a latent public (employees unaware their division is exposed), an aware public (staff who
    have read the rumour mill) and an active public (the works council and the journalists
    covering restructuring) — three audiences needing three strategies, not one press release.
    The <em>stakeholder</em> term marks the wider circle: everyone with a stake, whether or not
    they have yet become aware or active about any particular issue; mapping the movement from
    stakeholder to active public is situational analysis, and it is why practitioners speak of
    "publics" in the plural — the word is doing analytical work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practically, publics discipline everything downstream. A plant-closure case shows the
    craft: the regulator gets compliance-first notification and complete documentation;
    employees hear before the market does (internal publics are the classic first audience
    betrayed, and leaks are the standard punishment); the affected community gets the closure's
    consequences, the mitigation and the timeline; investors get the strategic rationale —
    four audiences, four framings of the <em>same true facts</em>, sequenced so that no public
    learns something destabilising from another's channel. The two-way discipline applies
    throughout: each public's channels are monitored (employee forums, local press, analyst
    calls), feedback is channelled into the decision, and the PR function acts as the
    organisation's <em>boundary spanner</em> — the role that carries the outside world's
    judgements inward, not just the organisation's messages outward.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Crisis communication is where all of this unit's ideas converge under maximum pressure,
    and it is worth sketching how the profession theorises it. Situational crisis
    communication theory (SCCT, developed by Timothy Coombs) matches response strategy to
    responsibility: crises the organisation clearly caused (a fatal product defect known
    about internally) demand full apology, compensation and corrective action; crises with
    ambiguous attribution (an accident under unclear causes) permit justification and
    context; crises the organisation is a victim of (rumour, product tampering by others)
    can be met with denial of responsibility — but matching wrong is catastrophic, most
    famously when a denial or a legalistic shrug meets evidence of organisational fault. The
    theory's working rules are the profession's distilled scar tissue: respond fast (the
    first hours frame every later story), say what you know and what you do not (speculation
    exposed as false destroys the credibility you will need tomorrow), centralise the
    message while decentralising the listening, and treat employees as a primary public —
    the workforce learns of its own company's crisis from the news at the organisation's
    permanent peril. The through-line back to this unit is exact: crisis practice is the
    symmetrical model under duress — research (monitor what publics are actually saying),
    honesty (the Lee principle), publics in the plural (each with its own stake in the same
    event), and outcomes (trust retained or destroyed) rather than outputs (statements
    issued).
  </p>
  <p>
    Three currents define the field's present. <strong>The critical counter-tradition</strong>:
    scholars of persuasion and power (following both Bernays' candour and Jacques Ellul's
    analysis of propaganda) note that real PR is predominantly <em>asymmetric</em> — advocacy
    for clients within contests (regulatory battles, union disputes, litigation) — and that the
    symmetrical ideal functions partly as professional self-description; understanding both
    registers is part of the education, because the ethical line (disclosure, no fake
    grassroots or "astroturfing", no undisclosed paid advocacy) is where practice and
    self-description must actually meet. <strong>Trust as the master variable</strong>:
    decades of institutional-trust surveying (the Edelman Trust Barometer is the standard
    reference) show trust migrating away from institutions toward "people like me" and
    employees-as-witnesses — which has quietly restructured the craft toward employee
    advocacy, executive visibility and credible action over polished statements (trust the
    deed more than the release). <strong>The platform transformation</strong>: when shared
    media can make any stakeholder a broadcaster, the news cycle compresses to minutes, the
    journalist-gatekeeper model weakens, and the oldest PR virtues — speed, accuracy,
    consistency across publics — become survival equipment. The definition, though, has not
    moved: strategic communication, building mutually beneficial relationships with publics,
    measured in trust. The course that follows teaches the mechanics — writing, campaigns,
    media relations, measurement — but every mechanic answers to the map fixed here: know
    your publics before your messages, earn credibility you cannot buy, keep the ethical
    line where the law and the code draw it, and count the relationship, not the clippings.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> PRSA Code of Ethics and Certificate in Principles of Public Relations content; Dietrich, <em>PESO model</em>; Grunig &amp; Hunt, <em>Managing Public Relations</em>.</p>
